# PHP Email Validation Function

```php
<?php

/**
 * Validate an email address using a regular expression.
 *
 * @param string $email The email address to validate.
 * @return bool True if valid, false otherwise.
 */
function validateEmail(string $email): bool
{
    $pattern = '/^[a-zA-Z0-9.!#$%&\'*+\/=?^_`{|}~-]+@[a-zA-Z0-9](?:[a-zA-Z0-9-]{0,61}[a-zA-Z0-9])?(?:\.[a-zA-Z0-9](?:[a-zA-Z0-9-]{0,61}[a-zA-Z0-9])?)+$/';

    return (bool) preg_match($pattern, $email);
}

// Example usage:
$tests = [
    'user@example.com',        // valid
    'john.doe@sub.domain.co',  // valid
    'invalid-email',           // invalid
    '@missing-local.com',      // invalid
    'user@',                    // invalid
    'user @example.com',        // invalid (space)
];

foreach ($tests as $email) {
    echo $email . ' => ' . (validateEmail($email) ? 'valid' : 'invalid') . PHP_EOL;
}
```

**Output:**
```
user@example.com => valid
john.doe@sub.domain.co => valid
invalid-email => invalid
@missing-local.com => invalid
user@ => invalid
user @example.com => invalid
```

## Notes

- The regex above is a practical approximation of RFC 5322 — it handles most real-world addresses without the extreme complexity of the full spec.
- For production code, PHP's built-in filter is simpler and more reliable:

```php
function validateEmail(string $email): bool
{
    return filter_var($email, FILTER_VALIDATE_EMAIL) !== false;
}
```

- Regex only checks *format*. To confirm an address actually exists, you'd need additional checks (e.g., MX record lookup or sending a confirmation email).

```php
<?php

/**
 * Validate an email address using a regular expression.
 *
 * @param string $email The email address to validate.
 * @return bool True if valid, false otherwise.
 */
function validateEmail(string $email): bool
{
    // Trim whitespace
    $email = trim($email);

    // Empty string is invalid
    if ($email === '') {
        return false;
    }

    // Regex: local part @ domain with TLD of 2+ letters
    $pattern = '/^[a-zA-Z0-9.!#$%&\'*+\/=?^_`{|}~-]+@[a-zA-Z0-9](?:[a-zA-Z0-9-]{0,61}[a-zA-Z0-9])?(?:\.[a-zA-Z0-9](?:[a-zA-Z0-9-]{0,61}[a-zA-Z0-9])?)*\.[a-zA-Z]{2,}$/';

    return preg_match($pattern, $email) === 1;
}

// Examples
