## More robust version

The simple pattern accepts a few invalid forms (e.g. `a..b@example.com`, `user@-example.com`). This stricter one splits the address and validates each part:

```php
function isValidEmail(string $email): bool
{
    $email = trim($email);

    // RFC 5321 limits: 254 total, 64 for the local part
    if ($email === '' || strlen($email) > 254) {
        return false;
    }

    $atPos = strrpos($email, '@');
    if ($atPos === false) {
        return false;
    }

    $local  = substr($email, 0, $atPos);
    $domain = substr($email, $atPos + 1);

    if ($local === '' || strlen($local) > 64) {
        return false;
    }

    // Local part: dot-separated atoms (no leading/trailing/double dots)
    $localPattern = '/^[a-zA-Z0-9!#$%&\'*+\/=?^_`{|}~-]+(\.[a-zA-Z0-9!#$%&\'*+\/=?^_`{|}~-]+)*$/';

    // Domain: dot-separated labels, no leading/trailing hyphens, alphabetic TLD
    $domainPattern = '/^(?:[a-zA-Z0-9](?:[a-zA-Z0-9-]{0,61}[a-zA-Z0-9])?\.)+[a-zA-Z]{2,}$/';

    return (bool) preg_match($localPattern, $local)
        && (bool) preg_match($domainPattern, $domain);
}
```

