## Basic implementation

```php
<?php

/**
 * Validate an email address with a regex.
 *
 * @param string $email
 * @return bool
 */
function isValidEmail(string $email): bool
{
    // Trim surrounding whitespace (common with form input)
    $email = trim($email);

    // RFC 5321 limits: 254 total, 64 for the local part
    if ($email === '' || strlen($email) > 254) {
        return false;
    }

    // Local part: dot-atom — no leading, trailing or consecutive dots
    $local = '[A-Za-z0-9!#$%&\'*+/=?^_`{|}~-]+(?:\.[A-Za-z0-9!#$%&\'*+/=?^_`{|}~-]+)*';

    // Domain: dot-separated labels (no leading/trailing hyphen) + alphabetic TLD
    $domain = '(?:[A-Za-z0-9](?:[A-Za-z0-9-]{0,61}[A-Za-z0-9])?\.)+[A-Za-z]{2,}';

    $pattern = '~^' . $local . '@' . $domain . '$~';

    $result = preg_match($pattern, $email);

    // preg_match() returns 1 (match), 0 (no match) or false (error)
    if ($result === false) {
        // Regex error (e.g. backtrack limit) — treat as invalid
        return false;
    }

    return $result === 1;
}
```

### Usage

```php
isValidEmail('user@example.com');        // true
isValidEmail('first.last+tag@sub.example.co.uk'); // true
isValidEmail('user@');                   // false
isValidEmail('user@@example.com');       // false
isValidEmail('.user@example.com');       // false
isValidEmail('user@-example.com');       // false
isValidEmail('user@example.c');          // false (TLD too short)
```

## How the pattern works

| Part | Pattern | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| Local | `[A-Za-z0-9!#$%&'*+/=?^_`{|}~-]+` | One or more allowed characters |
| Local | `(?:\.[...]+)*` | Optional dot-separated segments (no leading/trailing/double dots) |
| `@` | literal | Separator |
| Domain | `(?:[A-Za-z0-9](?:[A-Za-z0-9-]{0,61}[A-Za-z0-9])?\.)+` | One or more labels, each ≤ 63 chars, no leading/trailing hyphen |
| TLD | `[A-Za-z]{2,}` | At least two letters |

## Notes and caveats

- **This is a pragmatic pattern, not full RFC 5322.** It rejects quoted local parts (`"a b"@x.com`), IP-literal domains (`user@[192.168.0.1]`) and comments — these are legal but almost never used in practice.
- **For most production code, prefer PHP's built-in filter**, which is faster and maintained with the language:
  ```php
  $valid = filter_var($email, FILTER_VALIDATE_EMAIL) !== false;
  ```
  You can use both: `filter_var()` for the general check, plus a regex if you need stricter rules (e.g. blocking disposable domains or requiring a specific TLD).
- **Syntax ≠ deliverability.** A valid format doesn't mean the mailbox exists. If you need that, send a confirmation link or do an MX lookup:
  ```php
  $host = substr(strrchr($email, '@'), 1);
  $hasMx = checkdnsrr($host, 'MX');
  ```
- **Unicode addresses** (e.g. `josé@example.com`) need the `u` modifier and a `\p{L}` based character class; the pattern above is ASCII-only by design.
- Use `preg_last_error()` if you want to distinguish "no match" from "regex failure" (e.g. catastrophic backtracking on very long input).

