var_dump(isValidEmail('first.last+tag@mail.co')); // true
var_dump(isValidEmail('bad-email'));             // false
var_dump(isValidEmail('user@domain'));           // false
var_dump(isValidEmail('user @example.com'));     // false
```

### Notes

- The pattern covers the common cases: a local part of letters, digits, and `._%+-`, followed by `@`, a domain, and a top-level domain of at least two letters.
- It intentionally rejects quoted local parts, IP-literal domains (`user@[192.168.0.1]`), and comments — these are legal per RFC 5322 but rare in practice.
- For stricter validation (e.g., checking that the domain has valid MX records), combine this with `filter_var($email, FILTER_VALIDATE_EMAIL)` or a DNS lookup.
- The `^`/`$` anchors combined with `preg_match` mean the whole string must match. If you ever need to allow newlines inside the input, use `\z` instead of `$`.

## A practical regex-based validator

```php
<?php

/**
 * Validate an email address using a regular expression.
 *
 * @param string $email The address to validate.
 * @return bool         True if the address looks valid.
 */
function isValidEmail(string $email): bool
{
    $email = trim($email);

    // --- Basic sanity checks -------------------------------------------
    if ($email === '' || strlen($email) > 254) {
        return false;                       // RFC 5321 max total length
    }

    // --- The main pattern ----------------------------------------------
    // local part:  allowed chars, no leading/trailing/consecutive dots
    // domain:      labels of alphanumerics and hyphens, separated by dots
    $pattern = '/^[a-zA-Z0-9!#$%&\'*+\/=?^_`{|}~-]+'
             . '(?:\.[a-zA-Z0-9!#$%&\'*+\/=?^_`{|}~-]+)*'
             . '@'
             . '(?:[a-zA-Z0-9](?:[a-zA-Z0-9-]{0,61}[a-zA-Z0-9])?\.)+'
             . '[a-zA-Z]{2,}$/';

    if (!preg_match($pattern, $email)) {
        return false;
    }

    // --- Extra rules the regex can't express easily ---------------------
    [$local, $domain] = explode('@', $email, 2);

    if (strlen($local) > 64) {             // RFC 5321 max local-part length
        return false;
    }

    if ($local[0] === '.' || substr($local, -1) === '.' || str_contains($local, '..')) {
        return false;
    }

    // --- Optional: require a real-looking TLD ---------------------------
    $tld = substr($domain, strrpos($domain, '.') + 1);
    if (!preg_match('/^[a-zA-Z]{2,}$/', $tld)) {
        return false;
    }

    return true;
}
```

### Usage

```php
$tests = [
    'user@example.com'          => true,
    'first.last@sub.example.co' => true,
    'user+tag@example.org'      => true,
    'user_name@example.com'     => true,
    'plainaddress'              => false,
    '@example.com'              => false,
    'user@'                     => false,
    'user@@example.com'         => false,
    'user@.com'                 => false,
    'user..name@example.com'    => false,
    '.user@example.com'         => false,
    'user@example.c'            => false,   // TLD too short
    'user name@example.com'     => false,   // space not allowed
];

foreach ($tests as $email => $expected) {
    $result = isValidEmail($email);
    printf("%-30s %s (expected %s)\n",
        $email,
        $result === $expected ? 'OK  ' : 'FAIL',
        var_export($expected, true));
}
```

### Notes

- **`str_contains()`** requires PHP 8.0+. On older versions use `strpos($local, '..') !== false`.
- The pattern requires at least one dot in the domain and a 2+ character TLD, which rules out most typos.
- **Regex can't fully validate email.** It only checks *syntax*. It cannot tell you whether a mailbox actually exists — for that you need a confirmation email (double opt-in).
- For most production code, PHP's built-in filter is simpler and well maintained:

```php
function isValidEmail(string $email): bool
{
    return filter_var(trim($email), FILTER_VALIDATE_EMAIL) !== false;
}
```

  Use the regex version if you need stricter rules (e.g. blocking disposable-looking domains, requiring a specific TLD, or rejecting addresses with unusual local parts).

## Practical email validation with a regex

```php
<?php
declare(strict_types=1);

/**
 * Validate an email address using a regular expression.
 *
 * @param string $email The address to validate.
 * @return bool          True if the address looks syntactically valid.
 */
function isValidEmail(string $email): bool
{
    $email = trim($email);

    // RFC 5321: total address length must not exceed 254 characters.
    if ($email === '' || strlen($email) > 254) {
        return false;
    }

    // Local part: dot-atom. No leading, trailing, or consecutive dots.
    $local = '[A-Za-z0-9!#$%&\'*+/=?^_`{|}~\-]+'
           . '(?:\.[A-Za-z0-9!#$%&\'*+/=?^_`{|}~\-]+)*';

    // Domain: one or more labels (no leading/trailing hyphen), then an alphabetic TLD.
    $domain = '(?:[A-Za-z0-9](?:[A-Za-z0-9\-]{0,61}[A-Za-z0-9])?\.)+[A-Za-z]{2,63}';

    // The "D" modifier makes "$" match only at the very end (no trailing newline).
    $pattern = '/^(?P<local>' . $local . ')@(?P<domain>' . $domain . ')$/D';

    if (preg_match($pattern, $email, $m) !== 1) {
        return false;
    }

    // RFC 5321 length limits for each part.
    return strlen($m['local']) <= 64 && strlen($m['domain']) <= 253;
}
```

### Usage

```php
$tests = [
    'user@example.com'        => true,
    'first.last+tag@mail.co'  => true,
    'o\'brien@example.org'    => true,
    'user_name@sub.domain.io' => true,
    'user@example'            => false, // no TLD
    'user@.com'               => false, // empty label
    'user@-example.com'       => false, // label starts with hyphen
    '.user@example.com'       => false, // local part starts with dot
    'user..name@example.com'  => false, // consecutive dots
    'user name@example.com'   => false, // space
    "user@example.com\nBcc:x@y.com" => false, // header injection attempt
    ''                        => false,
];

foreach ($tests as $email => $expected) {
    $result = isValidEmail($email);
    printf("%-35s => %-5s (%s)\n", var_export($email, true), var_export($result, true),
           $result === $expected ? 'ok' : 'FAILED');
}
```

### A simpler variant

If you don't need the strict dot-atom and label rules:

```php
function isValidEmailSimple(string $email): bool
{
    return (bool) preg_match('/^[A-Za-z0-9._%+\-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.\-]+\.[A-Za-z]{2,}$/', trim($email));
}
```

### Built-in alternative

PHP ships with a filter that handles most real-world cases (and is faster than a hand-rolled regex):

```php
$valid = filter_var($email, FILTER_VALIDATE_EMAIL) !== false;
```

### Caveats

- **Regex ≠ deliverability.** This only checks syntax. To confirm a mailbox exists you still need an SMTP `RCPT TO` check.
- **Quoted local parts** (`"john doe"@example.com`) and **IP-literal domains** (`user@[192.0.2.1]`) are legal per RFC 5322 but rejected above. Add them explicitly if you need them.
- **Internationalized addresses** (Unicode local parts, IDN domains) need conversion first: `idn_to_ascii($domain, IDNA_DEFAULT, INTL_IDNA_VARIANT_UTS46)` for the domain, and a separate Unicode-aware regex for the local part.
- **Punycode TLDs** (e.g. `.xn--p1ai`) contain digits and hyphens, so the strict TLD class `[A-Za-z]{2,63}` will reject them — switch it to `[A-Za-z0-9\-]{2,63}` if you support them.
- **Never rely on this alone for security.** For account signups, always send a confirmation email; validation is a UX convenience, not an authentication mechanism.

```php
<?php

/**
 * Validate an email address using a regular expression.
 *
 * @param string $email The email address to validate.
 * @return bool True if the email is valid, false otherwise.
 */
function isValidEmail(string $email): bool
{
    // Trim whitespace and check for a non-empty value
    $email = trim($email);
    if ($email === '') {
        return false;
    }

    // Regular expression pattern
    $pattern = '/^[A-Za-z0-9._%+\-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.\-]+\.[A-Za-z]{2,}$/';

    return (bool) preg_match($pattern, $email);
}

// Examples
var_dump(isValidEmail('user@example.com'));   // bool(true)
var_dump(isValidEmail('john.doe+tag@sub.domain.org')); // bool(true)
var_dump(isValidEmail('invalid-email'));       // bool(false)
var_dump(isValidEmail('user@domain'));         // bool(false)
var_dump(isValidEmail(''));                    // bool(false)
```

**How it works:**

- `^` and `$` anchor the match to the start and end of the string.
- `[A-Za-z0-9._%+\-]+` matches one or more valid characters in the local part (before the `@`).
- `@` is the literal at-sign separator.
- `[A-Za-z0-9.\-]+` matches the domain name (allowing subdomains via dots).
- `\.` matches the literal dot.
- `[A-Za-z]{2,}` requires a top-level domain of at least two letters.

**Note:** This covers common cases but is not RFC 5322–complete. For production code, consider using PHP's built-in `filter_var()` with `FILTER_VALIDATE_EMAIL` as a secondary check, or the `egulias/email-validator` package for stricter validation.

```php
