## How it works

| Step | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `Normalizer::FORM_D` | Splits `é` into `e` + combining accent so the accent can be stripped. |
| `$map` | Handles characters where a plain accent strip would be wrong (`ß` → `ss`, `ä` → `ae`). |
| `iconv //TRANSLIT` | Converts remaining non-Latin scripts to ASCII approximations. |
| `preg_replace` | Anything not `a-z0-9` becomes the separator — this also removes leftover `?` marks that `iconv` leaves on some platforms. |
| `trim` + collapse | Prevents `--` and leading/trailing dashes. |

## Notes & alternatives

- **Extensions:** `mbstring` is required; `intl` (`Normalizer`) is optional but improves accent handling. `iconv` is part of core PHP.
- **`&` handling:** `AT&T` becomes `at-t`. If you want `and`, add `'&' => ' and '` to the `$map`.
- **Existing frameworks:** Laravel has `Str::slug()` and WordPress has `sanitize_title()` — both are battle-tested and worth using instead of rolling your own if you're already in those ecosystems.
- **Uniqueness:** Slugs usually need to be unique per record. Add a lookup at the end:

```php
function uniqueSlug(string $title, int $id): string {
    $base = slugify($title);
    $slug = $base;
    $i = 1;
    while (dbSlugExists($slug, $id)) {          // your own query
        $slug = $base . '-' . (++$i);
    }
    return $slug;
}
```

